[%
    title            = 'Ovid Among the Machines';
    identifier       = 'index';
    USE Ovid;
%]
[% WRAPPER include/wrapper.tt blogdown=1 -%]

In 1863, Samuel Butler looked at the machines of his day and saw something
evolving faster than we were. His conclusion, in [*Darwin among the
Machines*](https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Darwin_among_the_Machines): *"war to
the death should be instantly proclaimed against them. Every machine of every
sort should be destroyed by the well-wisher of his species."*

I disagree with the conclusion. I don't disagree with the observation.

I'm Curtis "Ovid" Poe. I help companies use AI to build software they can still
maintain in three years.

Most teams I work with have the same problem, and it isn't speed. The assistant
does what you ask, every time, and the sum of all those reasonable answers is a
codebase carrying debt nobody chose to accept. Skip the review and you're still
responsible — "human in the loop" becomes "human in the noose."

So I work the other way round: **engineering-driven AI, not AI-driven
engineering.** You see what the AI is about to do while you can still change it,
and you make the decisions. That is the difference between living among the
machines and being run by them.

